{
  "rationale": "The spec is area-minimising with a loose throughput floor (>=1 MSPS) and a moderate accuracy floor (max_abs_err <= 2^-10). Iterative reuses a single micro-rotation datapath, so it should achieve the smallest area for any given accuracy; unrolled_k replicates the datapath k times and is therefore expected to be dominated. We allocate 85% of the budget to iterative to densely map its Pareto front, focusing on the region where the accuracy constraint becomes feasible (roughly W >= 12, N >= 11) and extending to larger sizes to cover the hypervolume reference area (40000 um^2). The remaining 15% checks unrolled_k for any unexpected advantage, but we expect the final front to be entirely iterative. Ranges are chosen to include the feasibility boundary (slightly below the expected minimum W and N) and to cover the trade-off up to the reference point without wasting budget on clearly infeasible or dominated regions.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Iterative shares one datapath, so it has the smallest area per accuracy point. The throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) is easily met by iterative at these sizes (N+3 cycles/result, Fmax in the tens to hundreds of MHz). The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-10) requires at least ~12-bit data width and ~11 iterations; ranges start slightly below to let the search find the exact feasibility boundary and extend upward to map the area-accuracy trade-off up to the HV reference area.",
